      {"type":"mc","q":"Challenge (Advanced Language Conventions): A student must justify an answer using the example “The word “record” appears once as an action and once as a thing that is kept.” and avoid overgeneralizing. Which principle provides the strongest basis? Consider both the immediate evidence and the broader relationship. Choose the option that remains valid even when the context becomes more complex.","choices":["A word’s grammatical role depends on how it functions in context, so the same spelling can serve different parts of speech.","Independent clauses must be joined with correct coordination, subordination, or punctuation rather than a comma splice or run-on.","Verbs agree with their true subjects, and pronouns must clearly and consistently refer to appropriate antecedents.","Readers infer word meaning by using definitions, examples, contrasts, causes, and surrounding ideas in the passage."],"answer":"A word’s grammatical role depends on how it functions in context, so the same spelling can serve different parts of speech.","explain":"A word’s grammatical role depends on how it functions in context, so the same spelling can serve different parts of speech. The example is connected to word function, so this option gives the most defensible explanation."},
      {"type":"mc","q":"Challenge (Advanced Language Conventions): A student must justify an answer using the example “A sentence calls a plan tentative and then says another meeting is required before approval.” and avoid overgeneralizing. Which principle provides the strongest basis? Consider both the immediate evidence and the broader relationship. Choose the option that remains valid even when the context becomes more complex.","choices":["A word’s grammatical role depends on how it functions in context, so the same spelling can serve different parts of speech.","Independent clauses must be joined with correct coordination, subordination, or punctuation rather than a comma splice or run-on.","Verbs agree with their true subjects, and pronouns must clearly and consistently refer to appropriate antecedents.","Readers infer word meaning by using definitions, examples, contrasts, causes, and surrounding ideas in the passage."],"answer":"Readers infer word meaning by using definitions, examples, contrasts, causes, and surrounding ideas in the passage.","explain":"Readers infer word meaning by using definitions, examples, contrasts, causes, and surrounding ideas in the passage. The example is connected to context clues, so this option gives the most defensible explanation."},
      {"type":"mc","q":"Challenge (Advanced Language Conventions): A student must justify an answer using the example “A draft replaces vague wording with a precise verb and removes a repeated idea.” and avoid overgeneralizing. Which principle provides the strongest basis? Consider both the immediate evidence and the broader relationship. Choose the option that remains valid even when the context becomes more complex.","choices":["Verbs agree with their true subjects, and pronouns must clearly and consistently refer to appropriate antecedents.","Effective revision improves clarity, precision, concision, flow, tone, and emphasis while preserving the intended meaning.","A word’s grammatical role depends on how it functions in context, so the same spelling can serve different parts of speech.","Independent clauses must be joined with correct coordination, subordination, or punctuation rather than a comma splice or run-on."],"answer":"Effective revision improves clarity, precision, concision, flow, tone, and emphasis while preserving the intended meaning.","explain":"Effective revision improves clarity, precision, concision, flow, tone, and emphasis while preserving the intended meaning. The example is connected to style and revision, so this option gives the most defensible explanation."}
